  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nSaturday - Saturday Night...\n\nAnother slow-moving shortwave will cross the Ohio Valley region on \nSaturday. Ahead of the feature, deep moisture with ~2\" PWATs will \npool over our area, along with moderate instability spread through a \ntall/skinny CAPE profile. As a result, look for scattered to \nnumerous slow moving heavy showers and storms on Saturday. The slow \nstorm movement, high moisture content, and freezing levels over 15 K \nfeet will create efficient rainfall rates which will result in some \nlocalized precipitation totals of 2\"+ possible. 11/00z HREF data \ncontinues to suggest some PMM/LPMM data closer to the 48hr QPF max \nwhich suggests that higher end of the QPF envelope withing the \nensemble is possible. Something to keep an eye on. Highs on Saturday \nlook to range in the low to mid 80s.\n\nThe shortwave trough axis will push eastward Saturday night, \nshutting off the deeper moisture. Convection will diminish with a \ndry rest of the overnight expected. Lows should fall off into the mid \nand upper 60s.\n\nSunday - Tuesday...\n\nStrong upper ridging from the southern CONUS will re-center eastward \nover the SE CONUS through early next week, with increasing upper \nridging influence over the Ohio Valley as well. This will trend our \nforecast much drier along with increasing temperature back above \nnormal. Highs in the mid and upper 80s on Sunday will give way to \nhighs in the upper 80s and low 90s by Tuesday. Will also go with dry \nPops during this time.\n\nWednesday - Thursday...\n\nUpper ridging will stay centered over the SE CONUS through mid week, \nhowever this is a bit of uncertainty whether it will hold influence \nover our area, or weather it breaks down a bit allowing a surface \nfront to sink southward toward our region. The trend has been \nstronger with the ridge, and overall drier/hotter conditions \ncontinuing through mid week. However there is some data that \nsuggests the front drops close enough to us that we are able to \nspark some showers and storms over southern Indiana and perhaps \nnorthern KY. Will play the middle ground here and mention some \nisolated shower and storm chances across our northern CWA, but will \nalso keep pretty warm temperatures in around 90 degrees. If the \ndrier trend prevails, look for hotter temperature more solidly into \nthe 90s.\n\nExtended Forecast Discussion...\n\nThe month of September is off to a hot start.  Thus far we're \nrunning about +8.5F above normal at SDF, +8.6F at BWG, and +7.6F at \nLEX.  We're likely to keep those above average temperatures going \ndue to the expected warm period of weather for much of next week.\n\nLooking at the hemispheric scale, we're starting to see some \ninteresting changes on the horizon.  Quite a few of the extended \nmodels and ensembles are indicating a cool down for our area as we \nenter late September, probably after the 20th or so.  The forcing \nmechanism behind that appears to be a combination of factors that \nmay come together.  The Euro and its ensembles have been fairly \nintent on developing a strong area of high pressure over western \nAsia just north of the Himalayas.  If this occurs, we'll get a \nstrong pressure gradient there which will result in a good amount of \nmomentum transfer into the atmosphere.  Global atmospheric angular \nmomentum charts really ramp up over the next 2 weeks and this should \nlead to a Asian jet enhancement and eventually a Pacific jet \nextension and enhancement.  The downstream affects here would lead \nto a stronger ridge over AK and a +PNA pattern.  Downstream, decent \ntroughing looks to develop across the eastern CONUS.  Interestingly \nenough, the west Pacific looks to be favorable for continued typhoon \nactivity and we'll be watching for any typhoons that recurve and \nmerge into the Pacific Jet which could yield stronger impacts to the \nflow over the CONUS later this month. Considerable uncertainty \nremains as to how much the pattern will flex, but confidence is \nincreasing that seasonal fall air will be possible across our area \nfor late September into October.",
